{
  "id": "proj-rolling-welding-cart",
  "slug": "rolling-welding-cart",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Rolling Welding Cart",
  "summary": "A two-shelf cart that holds a welder and a gas bottle. Build the tool that makes the next builds easier.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 10,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Measure your welder's footprint and your gas bottle's diameter and height first. The sizes below suit a 140 A MIG machine and an 80 cu ft bottle; change the shelf size before you cut anything, not after.",
    "Cut the steel: from the angle, four 30 in and four 18 in pieces for two shelf frames; from the square tube, two 32 in front legs, two 44 in rear posts and one 16 in chain bar. Deburr every cut.",
    "Grind the mill scale off every joint area back to bright metal. Clean metal is most of the difference between a good weld and a bad one.",
    "Lay out each shelf frame with one leg of the angle down and pointing inward, so the shelf sheet will drop into it. Tack all four corners, check that the diagonals match, then weld them out.",
    "Hold the legs inside the corners of the top frame with welding magnets, flush with the leg tops, and set the bottom frame 6 in up from the floor. Tack, check plumb and diagonals, then weld out, alternating corners so the frame does not pull out of square.",
    "Weld the chain bar between the rear posts about 38 in up, weld one end of the chain to it, and fit the snap hook to the free end so the bottle is held by its upper third.",
    "Cut the sheet steel to drop into both frames and stitch-weld it from underneath, 1 in of weld every 6 in, so the thin sheet does not warp.",
    "Drill the bottom frame for the caster plates and bolt them on with nylon lock nuts: the two braked swivel casters at the rear posts (the handle end), the rigid casters at the front.",
    "Smooth the welds with the flap disc, degrease, then prime and paint. Leave one bare patch on the top frame for the ground clamp."
  ],
